x86_64-linux  clang emits only the C2 variant of a constructor or destructor it writes inline, where cxx1 emits C1 and C2 for every one - the difference implicit-move.nonames records, met here by every defaulted and written-in-class special member. And C's `C() = default;` beside `C(int)` is trivial, so clang calls nothing for `C c0;` and emits no _ZN1CC2Ev; cxx1 writes the defaulted constructor as a function with an empty body so that a class with constructors has one to call - identical behaviour, one unused symbol. Measured 2026-10-08 with VS clang 19.1.5.
x86_64-windows  the trivial `C() = default;` beside `C(int)`: clang emits no ??0C@@QEAA@XZ and cxx1 emits one with an empty body, as on the other targets. Every other name agrees. Measured 2026-10-08.
arm64-darwin  the same trivial defaulted constructor of C: __ZN1CC1Ev and __ZN1CC2Ev are cxx1's alone, and are the only differences. Measured 2026-10-08.
